  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on self-reported, subjective perceptions of mobility, norms, and social ties rather than direct behavioral tracking.",
      "what": "This study uses fuzzy-set qualitative comparative analysis (fsQCA) with a sample of 729 individuals to analyze how physical mobility, digital communication, social ties, and mobility norms interact to influence health-related quality of life, measured via the EQ-5D-5L instrument. The analysis identifies distinct set-theoretic causal pathways that lead to high quality of life across three age cohorts: younger, middle-aged, and senior populations.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that physical and digital mobilities do not act in isolation, but rather combine configurationally with social networks and social norms to shape individual wellbeing in a highly digitized society."
    }
